{
  "schema": "kaola-terminal-name-contract/1",
  "grammar": "<platform>-<PROJECT>-i<ISSUE>-<purpose>",
  "exact_platform_id_required": true,
  "short_aliases_are_not_platform_ids": ["claude", "cursor"],
  "advisory_field": "issue_name",
  "advisory_values": ["ok", "malformed", "platform-token-mismatch", "no-active-run"],
  "advisory_blocks_start": false,
  "name_role_field": "name_role",
  "name_role_values": ["worker", "host", "sideagent", "seat"],
  "non_worker_omits_issue_name": true,
  "join_rule": "name parses AND the repo's active run claims that issue number => bound; else unbound. Parses means classification ok: the name is <platform>-<PROJECT>-i<ISSUE>-<purpose> and platform is the exact platform id derived from platforms/*.yaml session_prefix (the id, such as claude-code or cursor-cli, not a short alias such as claude or cursor). The repo's active run is exactly one non-archived kaola-workflow/<run>/workflow-state.md in that repo's main checkout whose status is active, whose issue_number equals that ISSUE, and whose claim_repository_id matches the Runner record repository identity. A short alias, another platform's prefix, a malformed name, a host, sideagent, or seat name, no active run, a repository mismatch, and two active runs for one issue are unbound.",
  "advisory_note": "issue_name ok means classification ok and at least one non-archived active workflow-state.md claim for that issue_number, with a claim_repository_id present. It does not require exactly one claim and it does not compare claim_repository_id to the Runner record. Those checks are the join rule. issue_name never blocks start.",
